BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:IU Maurer School of Law
DTSTART:20261106T134500Z
DTEND:20261106T211500Z
LOCATION:LOWELL E. BAIER HALL/IU MAURER SCHOOL OF LAW
SUMMARY:ILJ Symposium: Evolving Frontiers in Criminal Law: Pregnancy\, Fa
 mily\, and Identity
DESCRIPTION:The Symposium originated from the book\, Pink Crime: Fighting
  Against the Criminalization of Motherhood\, Pregnancy\, and Queer Identi
 ty\, by IU Maurer School of Law Professor Valena Beety\, a founding board
  member of the Indiana Innocence Project. Pink Crime focuses on the ways 
 that criminal law is strategically used to limit the bodily autonomy of w
 omen and queer people\, combined with a discussion of how the system as a
  whole targets the most vulnerable populations while allowing bias\, stig
 ma\, and unreliable evidence to influence prosecution decisions\, especia
 lly in situations where no crime occurred at all.  This Symposium seeks t
 o expand upon Professor Beety's work in Pink Crime by bringing in expert 
 perspectives to illustrate how existing and developing legal mechanisms s
 trip away basic rights and lay the groundwork for more oppressive measure
 s in the future. This area of law is evolving too quickly for large porti
 ons of the legal academy to keep up. Therefore\, this Symposium aims to a
 nalyze the legal\, social\, and political dimensions of identity criminal
 ization and provide timely theory\, analysis\, and practical solutions fo
 r professionals to draw on as we move forward.  This symposium is offered
  both in person and online. Each panel has been approved for one (1) hour
  of in-person or virtual CLE credit by the Indiana Office of Admissions a
 nd Continuing Education. Please RSVP at the bottom of this page to attend
  in person. Zoom registration is available by panel below. ILJ associates
  are required to attend at least two panels. Attendance will be taken.Vie
 w the full agenda. (https://events.iu.edu/live/files/8496-ilj-symposium-a
 genda-publicpdf)ZOOM WEBINAR REGISTRATIONOpening Remarks &amp\; Prosecuti
 on of Pregnancy (Panel 1) Register for Zoom (https://iu.zoom.us/webinar/r
 egister/WN_W_4FzIBRRsaxZQWLx-O5Bw)Criminal Law After Dobbs (Panel 2) Regi
 ster for Zoom (https://iu.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_50EaYXAiTDOEA5ouDYn
 hkw)Family Regulation and Criminal Law (Panel 3) Register for Zoom (https
 ://iu.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_KP47CXL4Rn6GzrdNEjOSpQ)Criminalizing Qu
 eer Life and Family (Panel 4) Register for Zoom (https://iu.zoom.us/webin
 ar/register/WN_tM-sch02TdKdlIlNL5URfA)Systemic Failures Along the Path to
  Wrongful Convictions (Panel 5) &amp\; Closing Remarks Register for Zoom 
 (https://iu.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_yocH6mKWQd-hWRjkezuLzA)  We ackno
 wledge IU Research and the Research Conference Grant program for its gene
 rous funding and support of this symposium.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  The Symposium originated from the book\
 , <em>Pink Crime: Fighting Against the Criminalization of Motherhood\, Pr
 egnancy\, and Queer Identity</em>\, by IU Maurer School of Law Professor 
 Valena Beety\, a founding board member of the Indiana Innocence Project. 
 <em>Pink Crime</em> focuses on the ways that criminal law is strategicall
 y used to limit the bodily autonomy of women and queer people\, combined 
 with a discussion of how the system as a whole targets the most vulnerabl
 e populations while allowing bias\, stigma\, and unreliable evidence to i
 nfluence prosecution decisions\, especially in situations where no crime 
 occurred at all.</p><p>  This Symposium seeks to expand upon Professor Be
 ety's work in <em>Pink Crime</em> by bringing in expert perspectives to i
 llustrate how existing and developing legal mechanisms strip away basic r
 ights and lay the groundwork for more oppressive measures in the future. 
 This area of law is evolving too quickly for large portions of the legal 
 academy to keep up. Therefore\, this Symposium aims to analyze the legal\
 , social\, and political dimensions of identity criminalization and provi
 de timely theory\, analysis\, and practical solutions for professionals t
 o draw on as we move forward.</p><p>  This symposium is offered both in p
 erson and online. Each panel has been approved for one (1) hour of in-per
 son or virtual CLE credit by the Indiana Office of Admissions and Continu
 ing Education. Please RSVP at the bottom of this page to attend in person
 . Zoom registration is available by panel below. <strong><span style="tex
 t-decoration: underline\;">ILJ associates are required to attend at least
  two panels. Attendance will be taken.</span></strong></p><p><strong><a c
 lass="file file_8496 lw_files_pdf" title="ILJ Symposium Agenda - Public.p
 df" href="https://events.iu.edu/live/files/8496-ilj-symposium-agenda-publ
 icpdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View the full agenda.</a></strong><
 /p><p><span style="text-decoration: underline\;"><strong>ZOOM WEBINAR REG
 ISTRATION</strong></span></p><ul><li>Opening Remarks &amp\; Prosecution o
 f Pregnancy (Panel 1) <a href="https://iu.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_W_4
 FzIBRRsaxZQWLx-O5Bw">Register for Zoom</a></li><li>Criminal Law After Dob
 bs (Panel 2) <a href="https://iu.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_50EaYXAiTDOE
 A5ouDYnhkw">Register for Zoom</a></li><li>Family Regulation and Criminal 
 Law (Panel 3) <a href="https://iu.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_KP47CXL4Rn6
 GzrdNEjOSpQ">Register for Zoom</a></li><li>Criminalizing Queer Life and F
 amily (Panel 4) <a href="https://iu.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_tM-sch02T
 dKdlIlNL5URfA">Register for Zoom</a></li><li>Systemic Failures Along the 
 Path to Wrongful Convictions (Panel 5) &amp\; Closing Remarks <a href="ht
 tps://iu.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_yocH6mKWQd-hWRjkezuLzA">Register for
  Zoom</a></li></ul><p>  We acknowledge IU Research and the Research Confe
 rence Grant program for its generous funding and support of this symposiu
 m.</p>
